ge of I guess the range  of research is almost as broad as the  number the huge number of applications  for this particular product so while  we're in the AG department we often  think about agronomic research I mean it  goes from agronomic research to you know  setting cultivars growing conditions but  it also involves market research and  production so it's an entire array of  possibilities some of which we are quite  familiar with and some of which we  haven't traditionally been involved in  so it's a learning process on our part  one of the fun things about listening to  all these panelists is a lot of it you  sort of going yeah I learned that but  there's so much more to learn so from  the legal side it's a really exciting  area because it both exposes your  knowledge base and also your profound  ignorance without what can happen so  it's sort of like a lot of fun because  one a lot of the things we see and that  the department has done has been done  over time and it's a building effort and  we're dealing with structures that  already been created this is a new  opportunity it's a new program so you  can form it and mold it and try to make  it work as efficiently as possible which  is both challenging and is an exciting  so it's interesting for me hopefully  it's interesting and useful for you and  I can't emphasize how important it is to  get input from the folks who are there  in the fields doing the research doing  the growing and getting information  because I mean regulations just really  rules of the game so we try to create  certainty and clarity everyone knows  what's going on and you can perform in a  relatively easy environment they cost  what's required is clear and it's it  addresses sort of your needs so saying  all of that i'll give you a breezy intro  duction to what our program currently  looks like and essentially will look  like because it's really it's pretty  simple notwithstanding and the  challenges we're working in a murky  federal environment so you've got  transitioning where you've got  carve-outs under the federal farm bill  you've got additional supplements in  connection with the omnibus budget bill  which expands sort of the range and  scope of these activities but they're  not particularly fleshed out you've got  uncertainty at the federal regulatory  level when you speak to the agencies um  they're sort of tentative in their  approach because it's new to them and  the states are the ones who are really  developing these programs so in some  ways we have lots of opportunity but in  certain sense were also on our own in  trying to develop programs that serve  the needs of the industry and serves the  needs of public and that's sort of what  we're up to so essentially it currently  we've got a research program we're  accepting private partners we also work  with educational institutions but  generally our approach to both of these  entities is pretty much the same you've  got an application process the  application is fairly straightforward  and the information that is required is  fairly directed we obviously as required  by law require disclosure of the sites  we are looking for for research  proposals because ultimately our pilot  project projects our pilot projects for  research and study so when you know Dan  tells us you got to be thoughtful and  you've got a plan that's true in terms  of your approach to this agency as well  because what we're looking for is a  thoughtful plan as to what areas you  would like to work in and develop which  contributes to the knowledge and the  development of this particular industry  but it's research and study based and it  also needs to be appropriately scaled to  the project that you're proposing so I  mean we've had suggestions in our  regulations that agronomic research and  marketing research but but essentially  what you really need to  due to have a successful application is  to think about what you want to research  what areas can be developed 